now the United Kingdom moved into  lockdown as one nation but one of the  big questions about the future is  whether restrictions could be eased at  different speeds in different areas  Wales for example has already announced  a mild easing of the lockdown from  tomorrow their first minister Mark Drake  furred got ahead of the prime minister  with that announcement and he joins us  now from Cardiff thank you very much for  being with us this morning and before we  get on to the easing of the lockdown I  just want to ask your opinion on the new  slogan from the government stay at home  has been replaced by a stay alert what  do you make of it well I think it is  important for people to stay alert  because coronavirus has quite certainly  not gone away and is going to be with us  for a long time ahead but I also think  that if people are not leaving home for  an essential purpose for exercise or to  go to work then people will still be  staying at home because the fewer  contacts you have with other people the  more you suppress your own risk and the  risk to others so being alert is  important but staying at home has not  gone away so you do think that the stay  at home element should have been  retained well I'm just saying that the  message I will be giving to people in  Wales is is while they must be alert to  the continuing danger of coronavirus if  you're not out of your house for an  essential purpose and that does include  exercise that can include shopping and  it can must include going to work for  people who can safely do so staying at  home remains the best way that you can  protect yourself and others now you have  already announced a slight easing or  tweaking if you like of the lockdown in  Wales are you were you expecting to hear  something similar from Boris Johnson  later today well it's for the prime  minister to make the decisions for  England not for me to advise him but his  official spokesperson has been  emphasizing the Prime Minister's view  that maximum caution is still required  at this point so I'm broadly expecting  that the review of the regulations in  England will continue to have that  incremental approach to lifting the  lockdown rather than rather than  anything more dramatic  and Robert Jenner it was just saying how  he hopes that all the different nations  will move in a similar way I just wonder  how has number 10 been when it comes to  keeping you informed  I mean do you actually know what Boris  Johnson is gonna say later uh no I don't  know we did have a discussion with  Michael Gove on Friday which was helpful  in illustrating some of the thinking  that's going on inside Downing Street  I've described the relationship with the  UK government as one of fits and starts  when we do it when there is engagement  it is good and it is helpful and I wish  there could be more of it because the  more we talk to each other the more we  share ideas and perspectives the better  the chance will be that we can do what  mr. generic referred to and which is my  ambition as well I think we are stronger  when we move together when we have a  fundamentally shared sense of what needs  to be done and the more we're able to  discuss that across the United Kingdom I  think the greater the chance of  achieving that becomes I do wonder if it  is a issue that you don't know what he's  going to say and would it be problematic  if as the lockdown becomes eased further  think we have different rules on  different sides of the England Wales  border well I don't want to see  fundamentally different rules because  that is confusing for people and it  makes the basic message hard to convey  there will always be fine-tuning that's  the nature of devolution for 20 years  we've had different rules on either side  of the border and actually people in  Wales are very used to this but I think  of that as I say as customizing a basic  UK wide approach so that it fits as best  we can make it here in Wales I think  that's how we've tried to do things over  the last six weeks I think broadly that  has been successful that's how I am  committed to trying to make sure we go  on into the future we could have some  pretty big differences they frankly  couldn't we and we don't know for  example what Boris Johnson is going to  announce on schools but there has been  some speculation that he may relaxed the  rules on some primary schools but use  that the Welsh schools won't be  reopening on the 1st of June I mean  these are  fundamental differences well that would  be a significant difference but it's not  an unprecedented one our school holidays  are different in Wales to England now  shut down a well our schools are not all  shut down our schools are opened now for  children of key workers and vulnerable  children with more children attending  school since the easter holiday and our  schools will remain open for those  purposes as we build back the school  population here in Wales we will do it  in discussion with the teacher unions in  discussion with local authorities and  very importantly in discussion with  parents as well and we'll make the  decisions that are right for Wales they  may be in a fine-tuned rate different  from what's happening across our border  but the direction of travel the wish to  begin to be able to use schools for more  children and to return more children to  school I think that will be a shared  ambition so if you send your children to  school over a border which government  should you listen to well my own view  will be you should listen to the  government of where you live I know  there are some differences we do have  some children who live in Wales and go  to school in England and live in England  go to school in Wales and those parents  will need to discuss that with their  head teachers and with their local  education Authority it's not a large  number and those parents are very used  to dealing with those sorts of  ambiguities and they do it by discussing  their own circumstances with the people  on the ground who are best place to  advise them and just finally be devolved  administrations are responsible for a  health policy but it's Westminster that  it is largely looks after the economy  and of course is running these very  expensive schemes on following workers  or laying out loans to businesses do you  think that that focus on health is  partly why it seems in some cases the  government's in Scotland and Wales have  been more cautious than that in  Westminster on easing the lockdown  well I think that what is good for  people's health is good for the economy  I don't believe in this oppositional  idea that you could trade one against  the other  we do the right things by public health  we will avoid a second peak of the virus  and the worst thing for our economy  would be to have a rapid restart now  followed by a rapid clamping down as  things get out of hand so my view has  always been you must attend to the  public health dimension because that is  the way in which we can provide a solid  platform for our economy to get back to  work and it's very important for all  sorts of reasons people's health as well  as their livelihoods but that should  take place as well okay mark would thank  you very much for coming on the program  today thank you thank you
 

